#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716619 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34970073-a800-5eb6-81da-320b70a7dddb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716606} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e411c3e-85f1-573e-bc4d-fa950e446a0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5121e667-9ebc-5b11-8873-07fa428d0b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\cos\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716610} :answer-field/id #uuid "92591391-065f-5b2f-ab1d-2d2e3dea747e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b170817b-bc32-5678-95c6-e786e32f3dc6" :question/problem "![](images/50/50d0e14dc1d07ee58e2ede72997e6215c68b0d9d0b20492092e5e44ea85571c2.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $2$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$, that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at $J(4,0)$.\n\n$x=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$y=$ {{field-2}}\n\n$\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi)$." :question/worked-solution "An ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nHere $a=4$ and $b=2$, so\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta\\in[0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAt $\\theta=0$, cosine is $1$ and sine is $0$, giving $J(4,0)$. For increasing $\\theta$, the quarter-turn points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\theta=0&:\\quad (4,0)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,2)\\\\\n\\theta=\\pi&:\\quad (-4,0)\\\\\n\\theta=3\\pi/2&:\\quad (0,-2).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis order is counterclockwise. The interval $[0,2\\pi)$ covers one full traversal without including the initial point twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716657 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0048329b-3c2b-5752-ada4-f3ee93a82a0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(5 t^{5} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716650} {:answer/id #uuid "44f8c264-3170-541f-99b5-47d2deeaf143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(5 t^{5} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716661} {:answer/id #uuid "4a893e82-56ef-5c98-88c2-50031f003f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(5 t^{4} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716662} {:answer/id #uuid "6e3f4439-bfbb-588c-88ef-2c29a5851629" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(5 t^{4} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716670} {:answer/id #uuid "b1735c76-1077-5ad1-8b94-e09e82ff695f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(5 t^{4} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716676}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716676} :answer-field/id #uuid "32eeedbf-6290-5b3b-8404-6eecb506ace7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2331d05f-d03e-5a86-8a8d-86cf9c78a3d1" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/52/52c0369db51ccca8d99135a8c4814952780eba670829a2f66eb2d8be0c7cfede.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 5 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $\\frac{x}{3}=\\ln y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(6,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{3}=\\ln y\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,x=3 \\ln{\\left(y \\right)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle 3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 \\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{t}\\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=5 y e^{x}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=5 y e^{x} \\\\ &=5\\cdot t^{1}e^{3\\ln t} \\\\ &=5\\cdot t^{1}\\left(t^{3}\\right) \\\\ &=5 t^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 5 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(5 t^{4})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}5 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716659 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ccdd13-6da3-530c-9e26-3fba569ffd02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(2 t^{3} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716651} {:answer/id #uuid "53477f83-8194-5673-8d8a-d124ce23f63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(2 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716664} {:answer/id #uuid "60630512-4029-5e8a-806b-72639eac1a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(2 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716669} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5879e2-9647-53d9-beaf-c763322d551b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(2 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716673} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4128b1-a7a0-5e83-8ff9-7fb5aad10805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}\\left(2 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716669} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1dd5137-5131-5108-bbdd-186e15fc65bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "377cfa18-ce8d-5fe2-a8ea-5a6c9245dc31" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/52/52c0369db51ccca8d99135a8c4814952780eba670829a2f66eb2d8be0c7cfede.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,e^{2})$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le e^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le e^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{t^2}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{2} + 1}{t^2}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{|t|} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 y e^{x},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(e^{1\\ln t}\\right)t^{1} \\\\ &=2\\left(t^{1}\\right)t^{1} \\\\ &=2 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y e^{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}(2 t^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{1}{t^{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{e^{2}}2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc20370-81cc-5aa5-aa73-d9f3f36dc575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{3} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716655} {:answer/id #uuid "54e3da9a-d19a-57fe-8ffa-31de0dbe77f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{3} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{6}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716666} {:answer/id #uuid "6f6b4142-4629-5edd-b091-74ba0b531476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716671} {:answer/id #uuid "84f4f900-c4cd-5d67-9581-3729a5c67f57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(6 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716672} {:answer/id #uuid "e89650f1-4674-5bfe-a849-e63683522b53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{4}\\left(2 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716681}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716672} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fc1e1a9-d64b-53c1-9023-3739c87dfdf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3b5cab76-5120-5534-a8e7-a8349e068186" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/68/68d39defe7e4584c6b645163402cfbaefca793311f9849f8ee249bed57dc086e.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 2 x y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{3}{y}$ traversed between the points $(\\frac{3}{2},2)$ and $(\\frac{3}{4},4)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{3}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\frac{3}{t},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 2\\le t\\le 4\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $2\\le t\\le 4$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{3}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{t^4}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{4} + 9}{t^4}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 9}}{t^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2 x y^{3},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left(\\frac{3}{t}\\right)t^{3} \\\\ &=6 t^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 x y^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{4}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}(6 t^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{9}{t^{4}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{2}^{4}6 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 9}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716668 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ff1cbc-f670-536e-8d34-9f98665b03a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{3}\\left(3 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716653} {:answer/id #uuid "4c10ccc8-f423-501e-a71e-2fbc9f649165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{3}\\left(3 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716663} {:answer/id #uuid "53c0f1aa-5548-5115-aa36-dcf35eb91564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{3}\\left(3 t^{4} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716665} {:answer/id #uuid "56f7a11d-8832-5d98-b124-12b230c71e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{3}\\left(3 t^{5} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716667} {:answer/id #uuid "d8bd24e6-def3-5ad3-9efd-49ade4af128b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-2}^{3}\\left(3 t^{5} \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716667} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd2ff01f-11fd-5755-9ae9-7ddf11e973d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59b2f54d-9fc8-57bb-b332-5d62a2f03edb" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/07/07427c24e1419d2ea56169077343928792bd9116cb71cf46a9ad4a1662b914a6.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 3 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=- y^{2}$ traversed between the points $(-4,-2)$ and $(-9,3)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=- t^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle - t^{2},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad -2\\le t\\le 3\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $-2\\le t\\le 3$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(- t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=(- 2 t)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{(- 2 t)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=3 x^{2} y,$ then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left(- t^{2}\\right)^{2}t^{1} \\\\ &=3 t^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 x^{2} y\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{-2}^{3}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{3}(3 t^{5})\\cdot \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{-2}^{3}3 t^{5} \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716682 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efd0fce-e423-5998-b3fd-20eb4cba67d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716654} {:answer/id #uuid "2279feaa-43b4-5a79-81a0-0008d329df78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716656} {:answer/id #uuid "a14ccd94-3e1d-5736-abfe-d9e732f47c47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{6}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716674} {:answer/id #uuid "e160dc41-5742-5c89-be96-0cd29d54f8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(12 t^{2} \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{2}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716679} {:answer/id #uuid "e32aea50-4820-5ec3-839e-92669e36a968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{6}\\left(3 t \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716680}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716656} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96d3f7e-5b5f-5bb1-ab99-737abce170d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e942296e-f9c6-5796-a875-b8567d46c2cc" :question/problem "![Curve C with endpoints A and B and arrows along the path.](images/1c/1cc539b4c2ebce9ebcc17dc39e7c9dec840fe55cb5e4162aaad5719b400f33fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\int_C 3 x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s$\n\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{4}{y}$ traversed between the points $(4,1)$ and $(\\frac{2}{3},6)$.\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{4}{t}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle \\frac{4}{t},\\,t\\rangle,\\quad 1\\le t\\le 6\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 6$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r^{\\prime}(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}j \\\\ &=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t)j \\\\ &=\\left(- \\frac{4}{t^{2}}\\right)i+j \\\\ \\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{4}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1^2} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{t^4}+1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{\\frac{t^{4} + 16}{t^4}} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 16}}{t^2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=3 x y^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left(\\frac{4}{t}\\right)t^{2} \\\\ &=12 t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 x y^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{6}f(r(t))\\|r^{\\prime}(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{6}(12 t)\\cdot \\sqrt{1 + \\frac{16}{t^{4}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{6}\\frac{12 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 16}}{t}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "212c2921-d30c-57c3-bbea-b7a6621b0eb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 \\sqrt{3} + 32 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716689} {:answer/id #uuid "cf1868be-7916-5714-954c-369ca2de3a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{250}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716712} {:answer/id #uuid "da817805-7ee3-5a5a-940a-309d6c888e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "78" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716715} {:answer/id #uuid "e70e0dd7-8003-585c-a83a-be0ed35285b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{266}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716716} {:answer/id #uuid "e7977b4e-a75a-5dd8-94e4-b0c84bfb7380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{609}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716717}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716715} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6bfb20c-8eb0-5d30-b676-058d70e47a61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34f44129-201a-54e6-970d-29d57028a73d" :question/problem "![](images/68/68d8212cddf5e8b7d6d3036582d0cbbae1b37b67e1725781a2a78e1bc8a1fd17.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ between $(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{2},2 \\sqrt{6})$ and $(\\frac{\\ln{\\left(3 \\right)}}{2},\\sqrt{3})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $\\sqrt{3}\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=2 t^{3} e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=2 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $e^{-\\ln t}=1/t$ because $t>0$.\n\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}\\left(2 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\sqrt{3}}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}2 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $4$ and $25$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 2 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{4}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{4}^{25} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{250}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{16}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=78\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38395fe5-ee3a-508f-9dca-d1d1bc56427f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125 - 2 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716695} {:answer/id #uuid "6444413a-8648-5f12-81f4-c3cec79e3320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{2} - \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716700} {:answer/id #uuid "80f3c0cc-a6b6-5fa4-bc2e-967dd57598df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1 + 48 \\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716703} {:answer/id #uuid "97b801e8-cdb9-5575-9672-2e1fa3c5abe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716705} {:answer/id #uuid "fd94540b-67b1-5370-8402-8f5157373521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{125}{3} - \\frac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716695} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f846877-c953-5e54-876d-6af2265b8301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716691}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3961ef96-6834-5c4f-88b9-d15a61ff68b5" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a6f4294d412a929e06bb9d858983e171d4e0d5c422fcf9d68f809f1abd0a2439.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\ln{\\left(y \\right)}$ between $(0,1)$ and $(\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} + \\frac{\\ln{\\left(6 \\right)}}{2},2 \\sqrt{6})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{6}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\ln{\\left(t \\right)},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=3 t^{3} e^{- \\left(\\ln{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}=3 t^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nHere $e^{-\\ln t}=1/t$ because $t>0$.\n\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}\\left(3 t^{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}}{t}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2 \\sqrt{6}}3 t \\sqrt{t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $2$ and $25$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C 3 y^{3} e^{- x}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{25}\\frac{3 \\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[u^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right]_{2}^{25} \\\\ &=\\left(125\\right)-\\left(2 \\sqrt{2}\\right) \\\\ &=125 - 2 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e84e5d0-9ca8-5db6-835b-2061bc658465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\ln{\\left(8 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716697} {:answer/id #uuid "55ebeb45-3107-58c2-b552-f1798a783348" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716698} {:answer/id #uuid "6224a43a-9571-5240-b3ca-2921c9a64f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716699} {:answer/id #uuid "af99ec25-77ac-54cf-b754-a376e389bc87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716708} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dfe794-58c6-5fc2-aba6-52bdf0824c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716709}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716708} :answer-field/id #uuid "13ffc809-36b8-5f61-b6a3-dbe2b4425c5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d3bcdf3-0415-5974-930e-d7e742db72f9" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bffe2a9f729c630c79dac8a71c31d163546767a13cf00a032d28171d19f6e2a3.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=2 y - 5$ between $(1,3)$ and $(-3,1)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 3$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle 2 t - 5,t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2,1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2)^2+1}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{3}{- \\left(2 t - 5\\right) + t}=- \\frac{3}{t - 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(- \\frac{3}{t - 5}\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{5}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{t - 5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=5 - t$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $4$ and $2$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{3}{- x + y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{4}^{2}- \\frac{3 \\sqrt{5}}{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(u \\right)}\\right]_{4}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(4 \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=3 \\sqrt{5} \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBoth logarithm arguments are positive. The identity $\\ln A-\\ln B=\\ln(A/B)$ combines the endpoint logarithms.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716714 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f7ec94c-f72e-5d3c-8407-9c385ad3844a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{364}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716690} {:answer/id #uuid "33477e53-4b88-52da-a2f5-0464be374084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716692} {:answer/id #uuid "35c48841-ede4-595f-8e79-9f1e1bb31775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716694} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a53028-cb5f-5cfa-b712-ba666af0e44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716706} {:answer/id #uuid "a6f193e9-7e35-5f74-aa9a-c183396e61ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716707}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716706} :answer-field/id #uuid "1418ba9d-89a4-5d0b-921f-b42e9bb65550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "da642773-ea13-5c29-a39c-4b681f02440b" :question/problem "![](images/55/5561c5106c9e0e95b7dbe6f7d0b646bae7236c36291332aa1f410bf2946e7d53.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{1}{y}$ between $(1,1)$ and $(\\frac{1}{3},3)$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $1\\le t\\le 3$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle \\frac{1}{t},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle - \\frac{1}{t^{2}},1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{\\left(- \\frac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)^2+1}=\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSince $t>0$, the denominator in the norm simplifies without changing sign.\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right) t^{6}=t^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{3}\\left(t^{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}}{t^{2}}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{3}t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=t^{4} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=4 t^{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $2$ and $82$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C x y^{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{2}^{82}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{6}\\right]_{2}^{82} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3} + \\frac{41 \\sqrt{82}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65a6f03a-1691-509a-8932-adbd3b4d00d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "234" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716701} {:answer/id #uuid "71b20a76-506e-5302-83f9-28331b273500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "117" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716702} {:answer/id #uuid "c1da06a8-ee70-52a6-a2c1-c360f0e69818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{117}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716711} {:answer/id #uuid "d78c422e-b5d8-52aa-b107-dd33d4640ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716713} {:answer/id #uuid "eb94deff-1edc-59c3-aaee-929623f3a508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716701} :answer-field/id #uuid "196c4ed0-e61a-589b-b968-6adb40f87667" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f702e6ad-ae3a-5916-a6ab-b6277e28b6a6" :question/problem "![](images/21/21e7f28e62e188408bb3d5e5e535aed9ea735d0cdc9bb2b7c42ffc5d937b9837.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\int_C \\frac{4 x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=y^{2}$ between $(2,\\sqrt{2})$ and $(20,2 \\sqrt{5})$." :question/worked-solution "Set $y=t$. The endpoint $y$-coordinates give $\\sqrt{2}\\le t\\le 2 \\sqrt{5}$. Thus\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r(t)&=\\langle t^{2},t\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2 t,1\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2 t)^2+1}=\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nSubstitute both coordinates into the scalar function:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=\\frac{4 \\left(t^{2}\\right)}{t}=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMultiply by the arc-length factor:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{4 x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2 \\sqrt{5}}\\left(4 t\\right)\\left(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\sqrt{2}}^{2 \\sqrt{5}}4 t \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\nUse $u=4 t^{2} + 1$, so $\\mathrm{d}u=8 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; the new bounds are $9$ and $81$.\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C \\frac{4 x}{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{9}^{81}\\frac{\\sqrt{u}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}u \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{u^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{3}\\right]_{9}^{81} \\\\ &=\\left(243\\right)-\\left(9\\right) \\\\ &=234\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b781ee0-cf0f-58db-9a3e-fdbdcc8ec390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta+6,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716726} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa21782-774a-5208-abcb-7fd4284ab9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta+6,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716732} {:answer/id #uuid "6baae158-181b-50e6-afd0-431c0bef4f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=2\\sin\\theta+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716735} {:answer/id #uuid "8537e869-6f5c-5bd6-88aa-0f2aa9eaafc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716738} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5d0e4b-0d8f-5580-aff1-66efd2b44258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=5\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=2\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716740}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716738} :answer-field/id #uuid "c33ae772-985a-508f-a397-02c0078d05b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716747}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "12e8ae77-b3e1-5828-9b8b-966597109b14" :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/3b/3b7e6f3ad9d8198373fbc2f28198b80a4deeb02603a84c356792a1a2b14a230b.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $0$ and $4$; the vertical extremes are $-11$ and $-1$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{0+(4)}{2}=2, &k&=\\frac{-11+(-1)}{2}=-6,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{4-(0)}{2}=2, &b&=\\frac{-1-(-11)}{2}=5.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=2$, $k=-6$, $a=2$ and $b=5$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=5\\sin\\theta-6,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716730 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a97473c-a967-550d-a69e-4b9c214e387f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716724} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6761f5-0fd6-54b0-986c-bf7ea025672a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716729} {:answer/id #uuid "a27f17d4-f741-533f-a7bd-62adc00da7de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716742} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf504c0-b6ad-58bc-a5c1-469dfb9d0c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta-1,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716746} {:answer/id #uuid "c8808b99-77f5-533b-8576-d947dfc935c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta+1,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716748}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716729} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3bfff24-355d-549a-9f1e-22bc5227857a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4fc4204e-0344-5ebd-bc1f-b2502590e875" :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/7f/7f993f69b67f49ac59f934c67fa0d4117a3b554b4ba3652dc59e7da1ca8e3e2d.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-2$ and $6$; the vertical extremes are $-9$ and $7$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-2+(6)}{2}=2, &k&=\\frac{-9+(7)}{2}=-1,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{6-(-2)}{2}=4, &b&=\\frac{7-(-9)}{2}=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=2$, $k=-1$, $a=4$ and $b=8$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta-1,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716731 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b7140d-2fb1-5467-bc61-d3f414e99f85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716723} {:answer/id #uuid "2c625e3f-9ee8-5020-8798-20840eb0fb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716725} {:answer/id #uuid "470427a7-7b17-5111-8cfb-37407a935529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta,\\,y=2\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716727} {:answer/id #uuid "8126c79d-93cd-5e28-b87d-07f1e208f53f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=4\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=2\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716736} {:answer/id #uuid "cd8edce0-299a-5ee9-9de5-dc29022bbcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=2\\cos\\theta-2,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716725} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b8fbcb2-9e8c-52ef-8f01-b3947b931e72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "59714f8c-b360-55e4-9238-83e4407552fd" :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/52/521c3413e9421fd4e7552bc4eb7d3af19ce13d7363f050f2772ccb4a296ba389.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-2$ and $2$; the vertical extremes are $-6$ and $2$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-2+(2)}{2}=0, &k&=\\frac{-6+(2)}{2}=-2,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{2-(-2)}{2}=2, &b&=\\frac{2-(-6)}{2}=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=0$, $k=-2$, $a=2$ and $b=4$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\,y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8129cd29-8059-52bf-910e-b7ff631f2c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta+6,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716737} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6e9597-922a-51f1-b759-61260f5eda46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta-6,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716739} {:answer/id #uuid "a3da8927-116f-5e1e-b763-e152b3e11597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=6\\cos\\theta-6,\\,y=8\\sin\\theta+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716743} {:answer/id #uuid "e72501b7-b401-585c-ba3a-4c2925659ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta+6,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716752} {:answer/id #uuid "fa8139ef-e5c4-5ca5-ba17-e6db8fd449d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=8\\cos\\theta+5,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716752} :answer-field/id #uuid "edf5ff48-07a3-5548-a844-0246ea81c51a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a46ed07a-92a1-5847-92e0-d8970d04c9b7" :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/a7/a7d683f01ece53f00ba40f3c47fa8330dc02f30622552bf3a38976dbd9c0a5ff.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-2$ and $14$; the vertical extremes are $-1$ and $11$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-2+(14)}{2}=6, &k&=\\frac{-1+(11)}{2}=5,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{14-(-2)}{2}=8, &b&=\\frac{11-(-1)}{2}=6.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=6$, $k=5$, $a=8$ and $b=6$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=8\\cos\\theta+6,\\,y=6\\sin\\theta+5,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716750 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d0c3a2a-05fc-5bdc-87c6-bd7676d7021b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716728} {:answer/id #uuid "6997091a-aa0d-5a24-b844-2953eaaea897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=\\sin\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716733} {:answer/id #uuid "9de62df1-809d-5920-b835-f1623174e9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=\\cos\\theta,\\,y=3\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716741} {:answer/id #uuid "b9f9ee9b-a377-5745-b7dc-5b986cca1cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta,\\,y=\\sin\\theta-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716745} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbc53f4-b9d2-5cb0-a1dd-90039d73b021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x=3\\cos\\theta,\\,y=\\sin\\theta+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716751}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716733} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef72d6af-a3f7-5065-b8b9-e32aafd725f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "de59c734-5570-561d-9917-5fab0ec28dab" :question/problem "![Ellipse on coordinate axes with dashed horizontal and vertical diameters.](images/ee/ee41622e1810846b00fe003eb5dd264285fe31da94059a8293c21b60fb3e0cad.png)\n\nWhich set of parametric equations generates the ellipse shown, for $0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi$?" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal extremes are $-1$ and $5$; the vertical extremes are $-1$ and $1$. Their midpoints and half-widths give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} h&=\\frac{-1+(5)}{2}=2, &k&=\\frac{-1+(1)}{2}=0,\\\\\n a&=\\frac{5-(-1)}{2}=3, &b&=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAn ellipse with center $(h,k)$, horizontal radius $a$ and vertical radius $b$ has the standard parametrization\n\n$$\nx=h+a\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=k+b\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nHere $h=2$, $k=0$, $a=3$ and $b=1$. Substituting gives\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta+2,\\,y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716796 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe55b44-777c-59a6-8af6-225ad56484c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{147}+\\frac{y^2}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716816}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716816} :answer-field/id #uuid "e58631cf-37d4-5248-8949-e23562afba8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0cd7958a-cfcc-5757-8993-83fdbf6a828d" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=7\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=3\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$\n\nEnter only the left-hand side." :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=7\\sqrt{3}\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{x}{7\\sqrt{3}}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{7\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{147}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=3\\sqrt{2}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{2}}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{y}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{y^2}{18}&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{147}+\\frac{y^2}{18}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{147}+\\frac{y^2}{18}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716802 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1755c191-7e63-55d1-b695-91716675dbbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}x^2+\\frac{9}{16}y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716800} {:answer/id #uuid "1cb545af-0a2f-57ff-ae88-18fff6c3edca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}x^2+\\frac{25}{9}y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716801} {:answer/id #uuid "48ba6fc1-1f5c-5276-bde1-b5ee8ed15481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}x^2+\\frac{5}{4}y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716807} {:answer/id #uuid "5ca97abe-ddf0-5e8e-80d2-40d9d8d42e79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}x^2+\\frac{9}{16}y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716810} {:answer/id #uuid "680b44da-fd7d-54fc-b465-53710dda430c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}x^2+\\frac{25}{9}y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716800} :answer-field/id #uuid "e80925d7-c165-5124-82c5-659ff0373578" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1cdef8b9-f2d6-52e0-b2ca-9d0ed2ee8181" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{5}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{4}{3}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{3}{5}\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{5}{3}x&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{5}{3}x\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{25}{9}x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{4}{3}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{y}{\\frac{4}{3}}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{y}{\\frac{4}{3}}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{9}{16}y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}x^2+\\frac{9}{16}y^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}x^2+\\frac{9}{16}y^2=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0637ca3e-7912-52e3-97de-8315891a11e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{6}+\\frac{y^2}{6}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716795} {:answer/id #uuid "12b5e15a-08d0-59ec-b044-b72bfe2f15f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2+y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716797} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2c4fb7-6251-54f9-9f92-e36537fbee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x^2+\\frac{y^2}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716815} {:answer/id #uuid "e0858092-b506-5412-9063-9f3bfdf54948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{36}+\\frac{y^2}{36}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716817} {:answer/id #uuid "e5dc391f-cee1-5268-83d2-128429a9dac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{36}+y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716819} :answer-field/id #uuid "14e9a71d-3e23-5027-9d1d-e81a42a84b7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2c80883b-3840-5e3b-a4b0-7636f262a54f" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=6\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{x}{6}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{6}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{36}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\sin\\theta\\\\y&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(y\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{36}+y^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{36}+y^2=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716812 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29747ac3-b72a-5a23-ac0a-8867003f476b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25x^2+49y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716803} {:answer/id #uuid "35298663-11c9-5a90-a808-25f5ea72f8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49x^2+49y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716805} {:answer/id #uuid "400d1e24-f1cb-5e5c-ac98-1da42fea0853" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25x^2+25y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716806} {:answer/id #uuid "52e15e3c-4524-56af-ac56-20a746057175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35x^2+35y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716808} {:answer/id #uuid "5b34b2af-e062-52ad-9ba8-4321b819968f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49x^2+25y^2=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716809}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716809} :answer-field/id #uuid "1409103f-9b0c-5a6d-8e8c-4b5d18fea1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6be489fb-62c5-5fae-9d20-bfe6b1edc3a4" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{7}\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\frac{1}{5}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=\\frac{1}{7}\\cos\\theta\\\\7x&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(7x\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\49x^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=\\frac{1}{5}\\sin\\theta\\\\5y&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(5y\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\25y^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n49x^2+25y^2=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n49x^2+25y^2=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adc945bf-6d26-5879-af39-e16344a15d35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{80}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716814} :answer-field/id #uuid "83fe5ff2-2a0d-5c21-b996-73d64924b3de" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3e32c3b-5465-5295-af61-2d12b49256e0" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sqrt{5}\\sin\\theta,\\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation is\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$\n\nEnter only the left-hand side." :question/worked-solution "Isolate the trigonometric term in the $x$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx&=3\\cos\\theta\\\\\\frac{x}{3}&=\\cos\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{x^2}{9}&=\\cos^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolate the trigonometric term in the $y$ equation, then square.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny&=4\\sqrt{5}\\sin\\theta\\\\\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{5}}&=\\sin\\theta\\\\\\left(\\frac{y}{4\\sqrt{5}}\\right)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta\\\\\\frac{y^2}{80}&=\\sin^2\\theta\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd the squared equations and use $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{80}=\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1.\n$$\n\nThus the Cartesian equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{80}=1.\n$$\n\nThe full parameter interval traces the entire ellipse."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3035734-0085-5135-a2f2-18d62929903b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716973}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716973} :answer-field/id #uuid "7808619c-7ee0-5258-987d-7bf4842c0eee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716961} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b64c25-85fb-5a34-9187-9cbd5fc054bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716976}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716976} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e67c8b4-fbe7-58ad-8477-3da57159a8c9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9addc0db-d1fd-5bd9-9804-688f43894ff4" :question/problem "![](images/50/503814b9764dae553ac77b9db5fd393ea93cf02a86989afc59551b1f83ee32a5.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $6$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (3,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,6) \\\\ \\pi & (-3,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-6) \\\\ 2\\pi & (3,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the lower-right part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,-6)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(3,0)$ when $\\theta=2\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/4e/4e165eb4bd24aa6656234b1f4180e37ae090e929d1d56906d57e5735ab09c73e.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{3\\pi}{2},2\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c15dff8a-a7d5-5b30-95f2-fe2adc99f1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716968}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716968} :answer-field/id #uuid "7712aed7-0353-5c5d-8121-9891dbfa354c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716960} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc1396e5-ab5b-5067-af06-01c67cd4c24a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716967}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716967} :answer-field/id #uuid "f88d8284-7937-53a9-92b8-5488c01eb50a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce8a6bcf-e41c-5e7f-8e23-6720baf8c547" :question/problem "![](images/25/25239827db5874fa78a62a843d8775a2f619d9e73112bc28f9d5c9d3cda0792c.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $1$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (2,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,1) \\\\ \\pi & (-2,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-1) \\\\ 2\\pi & (2,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the upper-right part relative to the center. It starts at $(2,0)$ when $\\theta=0$ and ends at $(0,1)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/9c/9cc6d14cd425659b343c2e97ff9142f2a88e89862c494e9031bb2dbced4d6f25.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbb1f80-9393-5553-a056-d9ca4be18012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716959}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716959} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed5b49e-5f7d-5e67-8c21-92852b2939e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e67b2cd-3471-5649-874f-84e83ef85eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716955}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716955} :answer-field/id #uuid "96921b71-e692-5c07-93cb-5cf1ecf54bc5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d1b58eaa-303c-5a97-a706-9b408610b84d" :question/problem "![](images/70/70623a34ef6d5bbddee50ea91c0d640d83e08b8f1396222cd161987379498fd1.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=8\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $8$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (6,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,8) \\\\ \\pi & (-6,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-8) \\\\ 2\\pi & (6,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the lower part relative to the center. It starts at $(-6,0)$ when $\\theta=\\pi$ and ends at $(6,0)$ when $\\theta=2\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/3c/3c20a9561220717844184c0e5570e7838d6d237ae3ac9415d41a3a76161f65f6.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=8\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\pi,2\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cbc3d5f-ee27-5f7d-9cab-2edbc0f32807" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716962}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716962} :answer-field/id #uuid "49d8f0e9-0575-5328-a4cd-ca6e611ca268" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716957} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce3a1240-30cb-5b3a-97fb-ac985844f237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716969} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cdee286-db76-56ed-b49d-a21f481f1401" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d5444bbf-7006-55ec-9c8a-6b8f43564cdd" :question/problem "![](images/dc/dcaeffe2f84a5dfd04c64c9947b1240b0275241c5ab5c3276aa778a830ecba54.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=7\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $4$ and vertical radius $7$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (4,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,7) \\\\ \\pi & (-4,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-7) \\\\ 2\\pi & (4,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the left part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,7)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(0,-7)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/a7/a7543597e30e12a24ed370c2cafeae050ad74706b5718c048ec43463521f46aa.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=4\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=7\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e165c6d7-86aa-571d-b86f-652fce6e3fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716975}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716975} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f814a2-fb93-5b77-bda4-c734e30b78a3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716954} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfab0706-5b24-50ec-9f11-4c0e45f29d59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716971}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716971} :answer-field/id #uuid "3275f1dc-c12c-5ae1-abf4-073feabad060" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f83b95b3-fdc7-58cd-9179-e6fdfb5c0756" :question/problem "![](images/97/9711e8b2a21b2888a077a0c12dde13de2719ce314c1379b1319e4363df529e16.png)\n\nThe solid arc belongs to the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nFind the **closed interval** of $\\theta$ that traces exactly the solid arc once. Use endpoints between $0$ and $2\\pi$, including both endpoints of the arc.\n\n$\\theta\\in[${{field-1}}$,${{field-2}}$]$" :question/worked-solution "The ellipse has center $(0,0)$, horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $6$. The standard parametrization travels counterclockwise from the rightmost point. Substituting the cardinal parameter values gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\\theta &(x,y)\\\\\\hline 0 & (5,0) \\\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2} & (0,6) \\\\ \\pi & (-5,0) \\\\ \\frac{3\\pi}{2} & (0,-6) \\\\ 2\\pi & (5,0)\\end{array}\n$$\n\nThe **solid arc** is the upper-left part relative to the center. It starts at $(0,6)$ when $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and ends at $(-5,0)$ when $\\theta=\\pi$. The counterclockwise traversal between these endpoints follows exactly the solid part, including both endpoints.\n\n![](images/33/33bf8513f5b535844e3841f1f1c01a6ffd3718dda7cf0f609734d883306623e1.png)\n\nHere $\\theta$ is the angle on the scaled unit circle $((x-h)/a,(y-k)/b)$; it is generally different from the geometric angle on the ellipse. The required restricted parametrization is\n\n$$\nx=5\\cos\\theta,\\qquad y=6\\sin\\theta,\\qquad \\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\pi].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab8353f2-2e2d-5274-91ea-b4973113a352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716991} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d2a4ba6-55d2-579d-a04f-0aaaf2e3b391" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5536c7fc-1a73-5f25-8a70-b659d80aab4c" :question/problem "The nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ start at $O,$ with $|\\mathbf{a}|=8$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=6\\sqrt{2}.$ The diagram shows their counterclockwise angles from the same reference ray $r.$\n\n![Vectors a and b at counterclockwise angles of 30 degrees and 150 degrees from ray r, respectively.](images/70/7039bc30f707124fe8fa0a4167a0ef1979acaae13e0d39c2769c298f6af6bd8c.png)\n\nFind the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}.$ Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Both directions are measured counterclockwise from the same ray, so the angle between the vectors is\n\n$$\n\\theta=150^\\circ-30^\\circ=120^\\circ.\n$$\n\nThe vector being projected is $\\mathbf{b},$ whose magnitude is $6\\sqrt{2}.$ The direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ determines the projection axis, but its magnitude $8$ does not enter the formula.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}\n&=|\\mathbf{b}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=6\\sqrt{2}\\cos120^\\circ \\\\\n&=6\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe obtuse angle gives a negative scalar projection. Reversing which vector is projected generally changes the answer: using $8$ here would instead calculate the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716987 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "104d9ce7-5f5f-55b9-ad32-2122de5644a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716980} {:answer/id #uuid "149e830a-c28c-58bf-9836-7ae7a9b5d715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716981} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb2d4dc-87a7-58bd-aae8-3f4c975d5317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716985} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8c88b8-211f-5815-a235-e0f252d0ef28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716986} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e176b0-5113-586b-8966-f7f3ccbcde44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716990}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716985} :answer-field/id #uuid "430cd51e-8d61-5d6f-85d9-12ed802c5053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8566aa5e-1c2a-5be9-98b3-47321dbc4d02" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\mathbf{a}|=6$ and the angle between nonzero vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is $\\theta=60^\\circ.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}\n&=|\\mathbf{a}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=6\\cos60^\\circ \\\\\n&=6\\cdot\\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&=3.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe projection is positive because the angle between the vectors is acute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffb1e97a-1853-58e9-be0e-e47c1b1f99f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716992}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186716992} :answer-field/id #uuid "462b352f-ad04-539d-8fa6-9c46110a7c9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186716983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a10abc1f-f7ee-5b14-82fc-f6a39d006cad" :question/problem "The vectors below have magnitudes $|\\mathbf{a}|=8$ and $|\\mathbf{b}|=5.$ The dashed ray points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b};$ the marked $30^\\circ$ angle is between that ray and $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n![Vectors a and b with a 30-degree angle between a and the ray opposite b.](images/03/0378d71954ac8d6821e8db82e47a1ac90cebb9cbf4589b1edd1df26ba1bade2c.png)\n\nFind the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ Give an exact answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The marked angle is not the angle between the two vectors. Since the dashed ray and $\\mathbf{b}$ point in opposite directions, the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta=180^\\circ-30^\\circ=150^\\circ.\n$$\n\nWe are projecting $\\mathbf{a},$ so we use $|\\mathbf{a}|=8.$ The magnitude of $\\mathbf{b}$ is not needed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}\n&=|\\mathbf{a}|\\cos\\theta \\\\\n&=8\\cos150^\\circ \\\\\n&=8\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&=-4\\sqrt{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe negative sign indicates that the component of $\\mathbf{a}$ along the line of $\\mathbf{b}$ points opposite to $\\mathbf{b}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152433d4-98a7-597d-b01f-42113f46aeed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717021} {:answer/id #uuid "1ecbd050-8d73-5754-ac31-2fbcb871c727" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717023} {:answer/id #uuid "cf77acd2-fdff-5b34-85d5-2778ec3b88eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{21}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717033} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d57c8f-bc09-5a65-8445-c55601c12b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717035} {:answer/id #uuid "fbe3ac4b-cb76-5b7b-a716-57587ca3ce6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186717021} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d29c6d-688d-55d1-a288-edd17c8a67fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186717032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5155236b-cfa6-515f-b7c9-04f1be1b3778" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 1,2,2\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 2,-3,6\\rangle$. Find $\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}-\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Both projections use the same dot product, but their denominators differ:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}&=1(2)+2(-3)+2(6)=8,\\\\\n\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{1^2+2^2+2^2}=3,\\\\\n\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert&=\\sqrt{2^2+(-3)^2+6^2}=7.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}&=\\frac{\\mathbf{a}\\cdot\\mathbf{b}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{b}\\rVert}=\\frac{8}{7},\\\\\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}&=\\frac{\\mathbf{b}\\cdot\\mathbf{a}}{\\lVert\\mathbf{a}\\rVert}=\\frac{8}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract in the requested order:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\mathbf{a}-\\operatorname{comp}_{\\mathbf{a}}\\mathbf{b}\n=\\frac{8}{7}-\\frac{8}{3}\n=\\frac{24-56}{21}\n=-\\frac{32}{21}.\n$$\n\nThe dot product is symmetric; the scalar projection generally is not."}]}
